# GARDEN LICENSE — FOUNDER OWNERSHIP, RESERVED BUSINESSES AND AFRICAN UNION SUCCESSION

**Founder, original creator and declared rights owner:** Ankit Agrawal, resident of Indore, Madhya Pradesh, India.
**Copyright:** © Ankit Agrawal. All legally available rights reserved, subject to applicable law and binding existing rights.
**Amendment proposed:** 2026-11-25. Effective as a published license amendment only upon the founder's express approval of the exact wording and adoption on the default branch.

## 1. Founder ownership

Garden is the original project and design initiative of Ankit Agrawal. He expressly declares ownership of all Garden rights that he legally owns or controls, including eligible original documents, designs, specifications, theories as expressed in protected works, software, source materials, inventions, names, marks, domains, repositories, business designs and associated assets, across all versions, candidates, archives and future developments to the extent lawfully acquired.

While alive and legally capable, Ankit Agrawal retains sole authority over those rights, including permissions, licensing, enforcement, commercialization and disposition, except to the extent already validly transferred or otherwise restricted by law. Publication is not a donation, abandonment, assignment or open-source license. No claim is made to third-party rights, independent contributions not assigned to him, or abstract ideas or methods as such where the law does not permit exclusive ownership.

## 2. Permanent exclusion of the founder's food and fashion businesses

The founder's fast-food/food outlet and chain business (FOOD-001, including Aata Ghee Malai Pizza), and clothing/fashion chain business (FASHION-001, including Rang aur Roop), are expressly reserved to Ankit Agrawal. This exclusion extends to his legally owned or controlled business-specific designs, operational manuals, recipes as protected, brand assets, software, franchise rights, commercial arrangements, and related existing and future assets of those two ventures, including their business-specific implementations of Garden.

These reserved business rights are excluded from the Garden sale offer, the African Union succession, and all resulting community voting, licensing, control, revenue claims and disposition. Upon death they remain part of the founder's separate estate or pass under his separate valid arrangements; during incapacity they remain his property subject to lawful management arrangements. The African Union and community acquire no authority over them through this instrument. General-purpose Garden technology and BIZ-001 remain within the transferable Garden estate except for rights specific to the two excluded ventures. No implied grant of reserved rights arises merely from the use of common Garden concepts. The exclusion is controlling over inconsistent general language in Garden documents.

## 3. Succession to the African Union

Ankit Agrawal expressly directs and intends that upon his death, or upon legally established mental incapacity to manage his Garden affairs, all remaining legally transferable Garden rights other than Section 2's reserved businesses pass to the African Union (AU), to hold and administer for the benefit of and subject to binding decisions of the Africa-founded Global South Garden community described in Section 4, insofar as permitted by law and accepted by the AU.

The intended transfer is worldwide, for the entire remaining legal term of each right, including authority over use, modification, development, commercialization, licensing, proceeds, enforcement and disposition. It covers only rights still owned or controlled by the founder at the triggering event; completed lawful transfers and binding prior rights are respected. An uncompleted sale offer does not itself transfer title or override this succession.

Death must be supported by reliable official records. Incapacity must be established under applicable law by an independent, competent process, with qualified clinical evidence and appropriate legal determination, review and protection against fraud or coercion. Mere allegations, diagnosis, age, silence, disagreement, temporary unavailability, repository access or AI inference are insufficient. No self-appointed successor may exercise authority based on an unverified trigger. The legal instruments must specify whether and how rights return to the founder upon recovery of capacity; pending determination, no irreversible disposition on the basis of disputed or temporary incapacity is authorized.

This is a declaration of succession intent, not an executed will, trust, conditional assignment, acceptance by the AU, or automatic transfer of title. The founder intends to implement it through properly executed legal instruments while capable, with asset schedules, applicable formalities, AU acceptance and a lawful interim custodian if necessary. If acceptance or lawful implementation is pending, the competent estate representative or other legally authorized custodian should preserve the rights, not claim beneficial ownership or make discretionary dispositions contrary to this intent. No alternative permanent beneficiary is designated by this license.

## 4. Africa-founded Global South Garden community — collective decision authority

The AU is intended to serve as legal holder and administrator, not as an unrestricted independent decision maker. Subject to binding law and the accepted legal governance instrument, substantive decisions concerning all transferred Garden rights shall be decided through community votes open to every enrolled member on an equal one-person, one-vote basis, not solely by AU officials, governments, corporations, donors, technical operators or AI systems.

The initial community shall consist of 100,000 randomly selected eligible people from Africa, expanding gradually to 1,000,000 African participants. Thereafter it shall progressively include randomly selected eligible citizens of other Global South countries alongside African members, with a combined membership target of 100,000,000 and potentially 1,000,000,000 as eligible population, consent, practical capacity and lawful infrastructure permit. Africa remains the founding constituency, and African membership may continue to grow throughout expansion. All enrolled members, African and non-African, have equal voting rights. The AU remains the intended legal holder and accountable administrator, subject to the agreed governance instrument and the expanded community's valid votes. Selection and expansion must be transparent, independently auditable, fair across eligible populations, voluntary, resistant to capture and duplication, and protective of personal privacy. The implementing instrument shall establish a transparent and reviewable definition and list of eligible Global South countries; no single country may unilaterally determine membership. Eligibility, sampling frame, inclusion safeguards, replacement, voting thresholds, quorum, dispute resolution and phased expansion rules must be established in the implementing governance instrument; no particular country, institution or private actor may unilaterally appoint the electorate or capture the voting process.

Every enrolled member must have a meaningful opportunity to vote; results must be verifiable without exposing secret ballots. The community's authority covers all transferred rights, including licensing, commercial use, changes in ownership, revenue allocation, enforcement, governance amendments and Garden development. Delegated administration remains accountable to the community; material rights decisions require a valid community vote. The Section 2 exclusions cannot be overridden by community voting under this succession. Ownership or voting does not itself confer authority over any person or government, override human rights or local law, or substitute for Garden's technical validation, certification and deployment admission.

## 5. Existing sale offer — ₹20 crore net

During the founder's lifetime and legal capacity, Ankit Agrawal offers to sell the Garden rights he legally owns or controls, excluding the reserved businesses in Section 2, for **₹20,00,00,000 (twenty crore Indian rupees), net of all applicable taxes**.

After completion, the purchaser is intended to receive exclusive, worldwide, permanent and irrevocable ownership of the included rights for their remaining legal duration, with authority to use, modify, develop, commercialise, license, sell and transfer them. Third-party materials, contributions, open-source components and other people's rights are excluded unless lawfully transferable. The sale offer ceases to be available under the founder's personal authority upon a verified succession trigger; any later sale of transferred rights is a matter for lawful community governance. No transfer is complete until the final agreement is signed and all required formalities are completed.

Priority goes to the first eligible purchaser who completes both requirements:

1. The full payment required to leave the owner with ₹20 crore net reaches the nominated bank account as cleared, irrevocable funds.
2. The complete proposed transfer agreement and supporting documents reach the owner's nominated email address.

Reliable timestamps determine priority. An incomplete payment or agreement does not qualify. The owner must verify the purchaser and documents and sign the final agreement. The transfer becomes effective only after signing and completion of all required legal formalities. The final agreement must identify included and excluded assets, payment and tax arrangements, responsibilities, liabilities, handover process and applicable dispute procedure.

## 6. No unauthorized use

Nobody may use, copy, modify, implement, distribute, sell or commercially benefit from Garden's protected work without the applicable rights holder's written permission, except where permitted by law or binding existing rights. This includes indirect use and use through another person or organisation.

The rights holder retains the previously stated demand for **1,000% (10 times) of the total benefit gained from unauthorized use of Garden ideas**, to the extent recoverable under applicable law. This published demand does not by itself establish an enforceable agreement, penalty or entitlement to damages. The rights holder may seek orders to stop unlawful use, compensation and other legal remedies; where conduct constitutes a criminal offence, it may be reported to competent authorities.

## 7. Final conditions and authority

Before any transfer, verify ownership, third-party rights, purchaser or recipient identity, source of funds where relevant, applicable taxes, acceptance and required approvals or registrations. Existing lawful grants and transfers are not retroactively revoked. No patent grant or monopoly over unprotectable ideas is asserted.

The repository's default-branch LICENSE is the canonical published rights notice. During the founder's lifetime and legal capacity any change requires his express approval of the exact wording. After lawful succession, changes concerning transferred rights require valid community authorization under Section 4 and applicable law; the reserved business rights remain under their separate lawful owner. This license does not alter Garden's canonical technical source or independently authorize deployment.

